questions.text:
A random sample \(X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n\) has a probability density function
$$f(x, \lambda) =$$
$$\begin{cases}
\lambda x^{\lambda-1}, & x > 0, \quad 0 < \lambda < 1 \\ 0, & \text{elsewhere}
\end{cases}$$

Identify sufficient statistic for \(\lambda\).

Use matrix notation to fit a straight line $y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x$ to the following sample data by the method of least squares:

$$
\begin{array}
{c|ccccccc}
x & 6 & -4 & 10 & 8 & 4 & 13 & 2 \\
\hline
y & 20 & 18 & 6 & 2 & 13 & 22 & 5
\end{array}
$$

Write the design matrix $X$, the parameter vector $\boldsymbol{\beta}$, and the response vector $\mathbf{y}$. Hence obtain the least squares estimates of $\beta_0$ and $\beta_1$.

Use matrix notation to fit a straight line $y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x$ to the following five data points by the method of least squares.

$$
\begin{array}
{c|ccccc}
 x & -2 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \hline
 y & 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3
\end{array}
$$

Write down the design matrix $X$, the parameter vector $\boldsymbol{\beta}$, and the observation vector $\mathbf{y}$. Hence obtain the least squares estimates $\hat{\beta}_0$ and $\hat{\beta}_1$.
